Verification (The Litmus Test)
Run this command to see if the generated code is actually clean:

Bash
grep "save_env" /boot/grub/grub.cfg | cat -A
Good result: You see ^I (a tab) or standard spaces followed by the command.

Bad result: You see M-BM-  or other strange symbols.

Gemini said
Look at the first line of your grep output. Those leading spaces are still not standard spaces or tabs. In a clean cat -A output, a standard space looks like a literal space, and a tab looks like ^I.

The fact that your output shows that specific indentation without ^I markers—combined with the fact that it isn't executing—means those non-breaking spaces (M-BM- ) are still haunting the file. GRUB is likely hitting those characters and stopping the script before it can set the Windows default.

🛠️ The "Force Clean" Strategy
Since sed didn't catch them, we’ll use tr to strip everything that isn't a standard ASCII character. This is the most reliable way to kill "ghost" characters in a configuration file.

Run this command to purge the file:

Bash
sudo tr -cd '\11\12\15\40-\176' < /etc/grub.d/00_header > /etc/grub.d/00_header.clean
sudo mv /etc/grub.d/00_header.clean /etc/grub.d/00_header
sudo chmod +x /etc/grub.d/00_header
sudo update-grub
🔍 The Final Verification
After running the commands above, check the grep again. This time, we want to see if the command actually exists in the final config without the "invisible" wall.

Run this:

Bash
grep "save_env -f" /boot/grub/grub.cfg | cat -A
If the ghost is gone, you should see something like:
   save_env -f ($efi_device)/grubenv next_entry$


